# Data

A **resource** is a kind of thing the workbook keeps — here, the leads the rest of
the app enriches, scores, and ranks. You describe what it *is*; the database falls out.

The floor is just the shape. This alone is a working table — create, read, update,
delete, and **live** reads, for free:

resource Lead do
  name     :text
  revenue  :money
  status   :new | :enriched | :scored      # an enum, inline
  score    :int

  seed [
    %{name: "Acme",    revenue: 50_000, score: 70, status: :scored},
    %{name: "Globex",  revenue: 12_000, score: 41, status: :enriched},
    %{name: "Initech", revenue: 90_000, score: 88, status: :scored}
  ]
end

You name fields by **meaning** — `:text`, `:money`, an inline enum — and the type, the
validation, and how it renders all fall out of that. No type annotations, no guessing.

A pure value with no identity is a **record**, not a resource — it lives *inside* one:

record Money do
  cents     :int
  currency  :usd | :eur | :gbp
end

The vocabulary is **WIT's own**: a `record` is a value, a `resource` a stateful entity.
So the wire contract and the debugger speak the same words you author in.

The data is **server-authoritative** — the resource runs on the nexus. A `client` page
never holds the database; it holds the shape and calls the resource across [[The Dock]].
Next: [[Reads & writes]].
